# A `let` no line reads, in an unbounded function's body, whose right side calls an
# unbounded function and whose type generalizes (Semantics ch. 7: "The graph is lazy, the
# language is eager", so a function's `let` runs where it is written, read or not; a
# cycle point sits "at every call of an unbounded function from an unbounded function";
# Type System ch. 5: "A local `let` generalizes over the variables not free" in its
# surroundings, its color variables too). `Maker k` is a `u fn` for every color `u`: the
# call is one cycle of `Through`'s whatever reads it. Written by Claude on the
# claude-lowering branch; subject to change.
emit Ui.*
test mod Tests
use Ui.*

Maker: unbounded fn Nat -> (u fn Nat -> Nat)
fn Maker k = \|n| n + k

Through: unbounded fn Nat -> Nat
fn Through k:
    let Make = Maker k
    k + 1

@page: @unit
@page:
    !done <- @Run Through, 5                              # 0
    $shown <- "working" then "{!done}"
    Ui.Label($text=$shown)                                       # 1
